## III. Modular phase equations

For integer \\(n\\) and prime modulus \\(p\\), define the phase oscillator:

\\[ \boxed{\theta_p(n)=\frac{2\pi n}{p}} \\]

Its complex state is

\\[ \boxed{z_p(n)=e^{i\theta_p(n)}}. \\]

Define the phase distance from perfect alignment:

\\[ \boxed{\delta_p(n)=1-\cos\theta_p(n)}. \\]

Then

\\[ \boxed{ p\mid n \iff z_p(n)=1 \iff\delta_p(n)=0. } \\]

The exact modular resonance projector is

\\[ \boxed{ D_p(n)=\frac1p\sum\_{j=0}^{p-1} e^{2\pi ijn/p} =\mathbf1\_{p\mid n}. } \\]

This eliminates ambiguity from numerical trigonometric approximation.

## IV. Coupled wheel-phase system

Define the wheel survival indicator:

\\[ \boxed{ \chi_k(n)=\prod\_{j=1}^{k}(1-D\_{p_j}(n)). } \\]

Consequently,

\\[ \boxed{ \chi_k(n)= \begin{cases} 1,&\gcd(n,W_k)=1,\\\ 0,&\gcd(n,W_k)>1. \end{cases} } \\]

The exact recursive phase elimination law is

\\[ \boxed{ \chi\_{k+1}(n) =\chi_k(n)\left(1-D\_{p\_{k+1}}(n)\right). } \\]

For \\(n\ge2\\), primality follows at the square-root boundary:

\\[ \boxed{ n\text{ prime} \iff \prod\_{p\le\sqrt n}(1-D_p(n))=1. } \\]

## V. Collective interference equations

Introduce real weights \\(w_j\\), and define the collective complex phase field:

\\[ \boxed{ Z_k(n)=\sum\_{j=1}^{k}w_je^{2\pi in/p_j}. } \\]

Its Cartesian components are

\\[ \boxed{ A_k(n)=\sum\_{j=1}^{k}w_j\cos\frac{2\pi n}{p_j}, \quad B_k(n)=\sum\_{j=1}^{k}w_j\sin\frac{2\pi n}{p_j}. } \\]

The resulting amplitude and angular phase are

\\[ \boxed{ \rho_k(n)=|Z_k(n)|,\qquad \phi_k(n)=\arg Z_k(n), } \\]

where \\(\phi_k(n)\\) is undefined when \\(Z_k(n)=0\\).

The recursive interference law is

\\[ \boxed{ Z\_{k+1}(n)=Z_k(n)+ w\_{k+1}e^{2\pi in/p\_{k+1}}. } \\]

For nonzero rational weights, the field uniquely identifies residues modulo \\(W_k\\) in exact arithmetic:

\\[ \boxed{ Z_k(a)=Z_k(b)\iff a\equiv b\pmod{W_k}. } \\]

This injectivity is an information-preservation property, not a computational-efficiency result.

## VI. Global Fourier representation

The wheel indicator admits the exact expansion

\\[ \boxed{ \chi_k(n)= \frac1{W_k}\sum\_{h=0}^{W_k-1} c\_{W_k}(h)e^{2\pi ihn/W_k}, } \\]

where the Ramanujan sum is

\\[ \boxed{ c_W(h)= \sum\_{\substack{1\le r\le W\\\\\gcd(r,W)=1}} e^{2\pi ihr/W}. } \\]

For the squarefree modulus \\(W_k\\),

\\[ \boxed{ c\_{W_k}(h)=\prod\_{j=1}^{k}c\_{p_j}(h) } \\]

with

\\[ c_p(h)= \begin{cases} p-1,&p\mid h,\\\ -1,&p\nmid h. \end{cases} \\]

This establishes an exact equivalence between the multiplicative wheel and its additive Fourier representation.

## VII. Unified mathematical system

The Prime Atlas can now be summarized by five coupled equations:

\\[ \boxed{ \begin{aligned} W\_{k+1}&=p\_{k+1}W_k\\\\[4pt] S\_{k+1}&=(p\_{k+1}-1)S_k\\\\[4pt] z_p(n)&=e^{2\pi in/p}\\\\[4pt] \chi\_{k+1}(n)&=\chi_k(n)(1-D\_{p\_{k+1}}(n))\\\\[4pt] Z\_{k+1}(n)&=Z_k(n)+w\_{k+1}z\_{p\_{k+1}}(n) \end{aligned} } \\]

with initial conditions

\\[ \boxed{ W_0=1,\quad S_0=1,\quad \chi_0(n)=1,\quad Z_0(n)=0. } \\]

