# PRIME ATLAS — Why the Wheel Identifies Every Prime

The wheel and phase equations identify primes accurately because they are based on exact divisibility relationships, not statistical predictions.

The proof follows from the fundamental theorem of arithmetic: every composite integer has a prime factor no greater than its square root.

## 1. The wheel eliminates composites

Define the prime wheel:

\\[ \boxed{W_k=\prod\_{j=1}^{k}p_j} \\]

Its survival function is

\\[ \boxed{ \chi_k(n)= \begin{cases} 1,&\gcd(n,W_k)=1,\\\ 0,&\gcd(n,W_k)>1. \end{cases} } \\]

Every integer divisible by one of the wheel's primes is eliminated.

For example, the wheel \\(W_3=30\\) eliminates multiples of 2, 3, and 5.

