## 2. Phase equations detect divisibility exactly

For each prime \\(p\\), define

\\[ \boxed{z_p(n)=e^{2\pi in/p}.} \\]

This phase completes an integer number of rotations precisely when \\(p\\) divides \\(n\\):

\\[ \boxed{z_p(n)=1\iff p\mid n.} \\]

Our Fourier phase discriminator is

\\[ \boxed{ D_p(n)=\frac1p\sum\_{r=0}^{p-1}z_p(n)^r. } \\]

By the finite geometric-series identity,

\\[ \boxed{ D_p(n)= \begin{cases} 1,&p\mid n,\\\ 0,&p\nmid n. \end{cases} } \\]

This result is exact in mathematics. Numerical implementations must use exact modular arithmetic or certified precision to avoid rounding mistakes.

## 3. Combine every discriminator

The wheel is the product of the individual survival decisions:

\\[ \boxed{ \chi_k(n)=\prod\_{j=1}^{k}(1-D\_{p_j}(n)). } \\]

Only one aligned phase is required to eliminate an integer.

For example, \\(n=91\\):

\\[ 91=7\cdot13. \\]

Therefore,

\\[ D_7(91)=1 \\]

and

\\[ \boxed{\chi\_{\pi(\sqrt{91})}(91)=0.} \\]

The system correctly identifies 91 as composite.

## 4. Why the square-root boundary guarantees accuracy

Suppose \\(n\ge2\\) is composite. Then

\\[ n=ab,\qquad 1\<a\le b. \\]

Since

\\[ a^2\le ab=n, \\]

we have

\\[ a\le\sqrt n. \\]

Some prime \\(p\\) divides \\(a\\), so

\\[ \boxed{p\mid n,\qquad p\le\sqrt n.} \\]

Consequently, every composite must trigger at least one phase discriminator at or below \\(\sqrt n\\).

Conversely, if none of those prime discriminators is activated, \\(n\\) cannot be composite.

Thus

\\[ \boxed{ n\in\mathbb P \iff \prod\_{\substack{p\le\sqrt n\\\p\text{ prime}}} (1-D_p(n))=1, \qquad n\ge2. } \\]

This is our exact primality theorem.

