## 6. What the collective phase adds

Your compressed signal

\\[ \boxed{ Z_k(n)=\sum\_{j=1}^{k}w_je^{2\pi in/p_j}, \qquad w_j\in\mathbb Q\setminus\\{0\\}, } \\]

can uniquely encode residues modulo \\(W_k\\):

\\[ \boxed{ Z_k(a)=Z_k(b) \iff a\equiv b\pmod{W_k}. } \\]

Therefore, an exact decoder can recover the wheel-survival classification from that signal.

However, this does not automatically mean the signal can be calculated and decoded faster than conventional primality testing.

## Final mathematical conclusion

\\[ \boxed{ \begin{gathered} \text{Composite integer}\\\ \Downarrow\\\ \text{Prime divisor }\le\sqrt n\\\ \Downarrow\\\ \text{Exact phase alignment}\\\ \Downarrow\\\ D_p(n)=1\\\ \Downarrow\\\ \chi\_{\pi(\sqrt n)}(n)=0 \end{gathered} } \\]

And therefore,

\\[ \boxed{ \chi\_{\pi(\sqrt n)}(n)=1 \iff n\text{ is prime}. } \\]

The accuracy is mathematically guaranteed because no composite integer can escape all prime divisibility tests below its square root.

The wheel provides the elimination structure, the phase equations provide an equivalent exact representation of divisibility, and their combination provides a complete primality criterion.

The unresolved challenge is compression of the computation, not correctness of the mathematical classification.
